              {
                "text": "I'm testing whether the 8s fall due to gravity and get blocked by the 7 walls, checking column by column. Looking at column 0, there seem to be two 8s but the output only shows one, so maybe 8s positioned above a 7 wall get removed while only the ones below survive and settle against it.\n\nFor column 3, though, the wall only occupies row4 and both 8s appear stacked at the bottom, suggesting they pass through without being blocked. This inconsistency between columns makes me reconsider whether the rule is simpler than \"8s blocked by walls\" - I need to recheck the exact positions of 7s and 8s per column more carefully to find the actual pattern.\n\nCounting the 8s above row4 gives 13, plus 2 more below the wall, totaling 15 \u2014 matching the output's 15 eights across rows 6 and 7. So the free cells below the wall (25 total across rows 5-7, excluding the 7-occupied cell) get filled with 15 eights starting from the bottom, row by row.\n\nI confirm row7 fills completely with 8 eights, leaving 7 to place in row6, which fills right-to-left across columns 3-9, matching the output exactly. Now I'm wondering if this is really column-wise gravity, where each 8 falls down its own column until blocked by a 7.\n\nFilling row-by-row doesn't quite match either, since row6 leaves col0 out inconsistently. Comparing column totals before and after, the counts don't match a simple per-column preservation, so this looks more like a genuine flow/pour model where 8s redistribute across the grid rather than staying in their original columns.",
