              {
                "text": "I'm counting the 8s falling under gravity, but the input has 4 eights while the output shows 5 in that row, which doesn't match \u2014 trying to figure out if the eights fall per column instead, blocked by the 7s.\n\nChecking the first example, I confirm the total 8-count is preserved between input and output (15 in both), so the transformation just redistributes them rather than adding or removing any. The 8s appear to fall into gaps in the 7-barrier and pile into the bottom region, filling available cells that aren't occupied by the 7 shape, though the exact left-to-right packing pattern in the bottom rows still isn't clear to me.\n\nFor the query with only 5 eights and 8 free bottom-row cells, they land in columns 1-5, which isn't a simple left-aligned or right-aligned fill, so I'm considering whether the 8s might preserve some relationship to their original column positions rather than just stacking by count.\n\nLooking at the barrier row's holes at columns 2-3, it seems like the 8s might funnel down through those gaps like sand pouring through openings, piling up in a spread around the hole location rather than filling from an edge, which would explain why they end up centered near columns 1-5 instead of hard against a wall.",
